        margin="0.34,0.20"];

  prop [label="Propositional Logic\n(facts: true/false)", fillcolor="#EFEDFC", color="#7C74D6"];
  fol [label="First-Order Logic\n(objects & relations)", fillcolor="#EFEDFC", color="#7C74D6"];
  hol [label="Higher-Order Logic\n(relations as objects)", fillcolor="#E8F1FB", color="#3E86C8"];
  temp [label="Temporal Logic\n(facts at times)", fillcolor="#E8F1FB", color="#3E86C8"];

  root -> prop [color="#7C74D6", penwidth=2.0];
  root -> fol [color="#7C74D6", penwidth=2.0];
  root -> hol [color="#3E86C8", penwidth=2.0];
  root -> temp [color="#3E86C8", penwidth=2.0];
}
```

::::
:::

* Epistemological Commitment

- @Definition@: **Epistemological commitment** is the possible states of knowledge an
  _agent_ holds with respect to _each fact_

- @Examples@
  - _Propositional_, _first-order logic_: three possible states of belief
    - I.e., true, false, unknown
  - _Probability theory_: each sentence has a degree of belief in [0, 1]
    - E.g., $\Pr(X = 6) = 0.3$
  - _Temporal logic_: facts hold at specific times or intervals
    - E.g., _"The light is ON at time $t_1$ and OFF at time $t_2$"_

* Non-monotonic Logic

- @Definition@: **Non-monotonic logic** is a logic where adding new information
  can _invalidate previous conclusions_ `#cite("mccarthy1980circumscription")`{=typst}

- @Comparison@:
  - _Classical logic_: once proven, conclusions remain true even with new
    information
  - _Non-monotonic logic_: conclusions change as new facts are learned

- @Intuition@:
  - Real-world reasoning involves incomplete or evolving knowledge
  - Non-monotonic logic allows systems to reason flexibly and adapt to new
    circumstances

- @Example@
  - Initial KB: _"Birds fly"_, _"Tweety is a bird"_
  - Conclusion: _"Tweety can fly"_
  - New fact: _"Tweety is a penguin"_ and _"Penguins cannot fly"_
  - Revised conclusion: _"Tweety cannot fly"_

* Default Reasoning

- @Definition@: **Default reasoning** makes _assumptions by default_ in the absence
  of contrary evidence, allowing conclusions based on typical situations unless
  exceptions are found `#cite("reiter1980default")`{=typst}

- @Key idea@:
  - Assume the most likely case unless specified otherwise
  - If new information contradicts the assumption, revise the conclusion
  - Allow systems to function reasonably without complete information

- @Example@
  - Default rule: _"Typically, birds can fly"_
  - Fact: _"Tweety is a bird"_
  - Conclusion: _"Tweety can fly"_
  - New fact: _"Tweety is a penguin"_
  - Revised: _"Tweety cannot fly"_

* Non-Monotonic Logic: University Example

- @Initial facts@
  - $Alice$ is a $Student$
  - $Alice$ belongs to the $ComputerScience$ department
  - $CS101$ is a $Course$ offered by the $ComputerScience$ department

- @Default rule@:
  - Each $Student$ in the $ComputerScience$ department takes all classes in their
    department

- @Initial reasoning@
  - Since $Alice$ is a $Student$ in $ComputerScience$, by default $Students$
    take $CS101$
  - Conclusion: $takesCourse(Alice, CS101)$

- @New information@
  - $Alice$ is a student who does not meet the prerequisites for $CS101$

- @Revised reasoning@
  - Conclusion: $\lnot takesCourse(Alice, CS101)$

* Common Sense Reasoning

- @Problem@: Typical knowledge is often implicit and taken by granted by humans
  - _"If you drop a glass, it will likely break"_
  - _"People eat food when hungry"_

- @Definition@: **Common sense reasoning** makes assumptions and draws
  conclusions based on everyday knowledge about the world

  - @Pros@
    - Deals with incomplete, uncertain, ambiguous information
    - Relies on defaults, heuristics, and typical patterns, not strict proofs
    - Flexible and tolerant of exceptions

  - @Cons@
    - Knowledge is vast, informal, imprecisely defined
    - Difficult to encode in machine-readable form
    - Handling exceptions and contradictions is complex

- @Techniques@
  - Knowledge graphs, non-monotonic logic, probabilistic reasoning, machine learning

* Common Sense Reasoning: Restaurant Example

- @Initial facts@
  - $Bob$ enters a $Restaurant$
  - $Bob$ sits at a $Table$

- @Common sense knowledge@
  - $Customers$ who sit $\implies$ intend to order
  - $Waiters$ bring $Menu$ before taking order
  - $Customers$ pay before leaving

- @Reasoning steps@
  - $Bob$ sat at table $\implies$ intends to eat
  - Therefore, $Bob$ will get menu and order

- @New information@
  - $Bob$ asks for directions only
  - Revise: $Bob$ will NOT order

* Open World vs Closed World Assumptions

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Aspect", "Closed World Assumption (CWA)", "Open World Assumption (OWA)"),
  rows: (
    ("Missing info", "False by default", "Unknown (not false)"),
    ("Example: Bob takes CS101?", "False", "Unknown"),
    ("DB systems", "Relational DB, logic programs", "Semantic Web"),
    ("Best for", "Complete, static knowledge", "Incomplete, evolving data"),
  ),
)
```

- @Example CWA@:
  - Fact: _"Alice takes CS101"_ is in the database
  - Nothing said about Bob
  - Conclusion: _"Bob does not take CS101"_ (assumed false)

- @Example OWA@:
  - Fact: _"Alice takes CS101"_ is known
  - Nothing said about Bob
  - Conclusion: _"Bob's enrollment is unknown"_ (could be true or false)

* Inductive Logic Programming

- @Definition@: **Inductive logic programming** learns logical rules from examples
  and background knowledge

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

- @Example@
  - Background: _"Birds have wings"_
  - Positive: _"Tweety is a bird that can fly"_
  - Negative: _"Penguin cannot fly"_
  - Learned: _"Birds fly unless penguins"_

::::
:::: {.column width=60%}

```graphviz
digraph ILP {
  graph [rankdir=LR, splines=curved, bgcolor="transparent",
         ranksep="1.1 equally", nodesep=0.26, pad=0.4, fontname="Helvetica"];
  node  [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=14,
         fontcolor="#26215C", color="#D8D6EE", penwidth=1.2,
         height=0.52, margin="0.24,0.10"];
  edge  [arrowhead=vee, penwidth=1.6, color="#B9B6D6"];

  background [label="Background Knowledge",
              fillcolor="#FBEEE2", color="#C07A45"];
  pos_ex [label="Positive Examples",
          fillcolor="#E5F4EE", color="#2F9678"];
  neg_ex [label="Negative Examples",
          fillcolor="#FFF3E0", color="#D97706"];
  hypothesis [label="Hypothesis Space", fillcolor="#EFEDFC", color="#7C74D6"];
  learned_rule [label="Learned Rules", fillcolor="#E8F1FB", color="#3E86C8"];

  background -> hypothesis [color="#C07A45", penwidth=2.0];
  pos_ex -> hypothesis [color="#2F9678", penwidth=2.0];
  neg_ex -> hypothesis [color="#D97706", penwidth=2.0];
  hypothesis -> learned_rule [color="#3E86C8", penwidth=2.0];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Pros@:
  - Produces human-readable rules
  - Integrates learning with reasoning
  - Supports background knowledge

- @Challenges@:
  - Complexity with large datasets
  - Cannot handle noisy data

# Knowledge Representation Frameworks

## Description Logics

// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Description_logic

* Description Logic

- @Definition@: **Description logic** represents structured knowledge about a
  domain `#cite("baader2003dlhandbook")`{=typst}
  - Balances expressivity and computational efficiency
  - More expressive than propositional logic, less than first-order logic
  - Used widely in ontologies (e.g., OWL)

- @Components@
  - _Classes_: abstract groups (e.g., $Person$, $Animal$)
  - _Properties_: binary relations (e.g., $hasChild$, $ownsPet$)
  - _Instances_: specific objects (e.g., $GP$, $Nuvolo$)

- @Syntax@: combines atomic concepts and roles with logical constructors
  ($\sqcap$, $\sqcup$, $\neg$, $\forall$, $\exists$)
  - E.g., $Father \equiv Man \sqcap \exists hasChild.Person$

- @Reasoning tasks@
  - Concept subsumption: _"Is $A$ a subset of $B$?"_
  - Instance checking: _"Does $a$ belong to $A$?"_

* ALC

- @Definition@: **Attributive concept Language with Complements (ALC)** is a
  basic but expressive _description logic_

- Combines logical operators
  - $\sqcap$ (and), $\sqcup$ (or), $\neg$ (not)
  - Existential and universal quantification: $\exists R.C$, $\forall R.C$
  - Set-theoretic interpretation (e.g., classes as sets, properties as binary
    relations)

- @Example@
  - _"All students take some course"_:
    $\text{Student} \equiv \exists \text{takes}.\text{Course}$
  - _"A mother is a woman with at least one child"_:
    $\text{Mother} \equiv \text{Woman} \sqcap \exists \text{hasChild}.\top$

- @Properties@
  - Decidable
  - Balances expressiveness and computational complexity
  - Foundation for more complex logics in OWL

* SHOIN

- @Definition@: **SHOIN** is a description logic more expressive than ALC
  `#cite("horrocks2003owl")`{=typst}

- @Components@:
  - $\mathcal{S}$: transitive properties (e.g., $ancestorOf$)
  - $\mathcal{H}$: role hierarchies (e.g., $hasSon \sqsubseteq hasChild$)
  - $\mathcal{O}$: specific individuals (e.g., $John$ nominal)
  - $\mathcal{I}$: inverse roles (e.g., $isChildOf$ inverse $hasChild$)
  - $\mathcal{N}$: cardinality constraints (e.g., _"exactly 1"_)

- @Example@: _"A person has exactly two children"_:
  $Person \sqsubseteq (=2\ hasChild.\top)$

- @Pros@
  - More powerful than ALC, but harder reasoning
  - Models richer real-world scenarios
  - Foundation for OWL DL

// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Ontology_Language

* OWL

- @Definition@: **Web Ontology Language (OWL)** `#cite("mcguinness2004owl")`{=typst}
  - Semantic web language for complex knowledge
  - Rich representation on the web (based on SHOIN)
  - Formal semantics for machine reasoning

- @Components@: classes, properties, individuals, axioms

- @Example@: _"Every cat is a mammal"_: $Cat \sqsubseteq Mammal$

- @Applications@: semantic search, biomedical data

- @Variants@:
  - _OWL Lite_: simpler, for classification hierarchies
  - _OWL DL_: full expressiveness with decidable reasoning
  - _OWL Full_: maximum expressiveness, undecidable

* Example of OWL in RDF

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
